  two answers share almost no code.

  - [1mBrowser-local ([38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m)[0m — buys you a working feature with zero backend and zero dependencies; costs you that
    preferences are per-browser-per-device, so the same user on their phone sees defaults. Rules out nothing
    permanently: a server-backed layer can be added behind the same read/write interface later.
  - [1mServer-backed per user account[0m — buys real cross-device persistence tied to identity; costs a backend, a datastore,
    an auth story, and a real [38;5;153mlogin()[39m instead of the current stub. This repo has none of that, so it's a much larger
    project.
  - [1mNode-side config file[0m — only makes sense if the preferences belong to the [38;5;153msrc/[39m CLI half, not the login page. Cheap,
    but it's a different feature than what the webapp would need.

  [1mMy recommendation: browser-local [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m,[0m with the storage access wrapped in a small module so the backing store
  is swappable. The login flow is a stub, so there is no real user identity to key server-side preferences to yet —
  building the server path now would mean inventing an auth system this brainstorm didn't ask for.

